## Nmap Scan Results

Conducted an external reconnaissance scan using Nmap.

### Findings
- Port 22 (SSH) open — OpenSSH 8.7
- Port 80 (HTTP) open — Apache httpd 2.4.66 (Amazon Linux)
- 998 ports filtered (no response)

### Interpretation
The system exposes only required services. Security groups effectively block all other ports, reducing the attack surface.

### Security Insight
Service version detection reveals potential targets for vulnerability analysis, emphasizing the importance of patching and minimizing exposed services.

## Unauthorized Access Simulation

Performed multiple failed SSH login attempts.

### Findings
- Access attempts were denied
- No unauthorized access was granted

### CloudTrail Observation
SSH login attempts were not recorded in CloudTrail.

### Insight
CloudTrail logs AWS API activity but does not capture OS-level authentication attempts, highlighting a visibility gap.

## Attack Simulation Summary

Simulated external reconnaissance and unauthorized access attempts against a public-facing EC2 instance.

### Key Observations

- Only intended services (SSH and HTTP) were exposed
- Security groups effectively filtered all other ports
- Unauthorized SSH access attempts were denied
- CloudTrail did not capture OS-level login attempts
- CloudWatch provided limited visibility into these events

### Interpretation

The infrastructure was properly secured at the network level, but lacked deep visibility into host-level activity.

This demonstrates a common gap in cloud environments where infrastructure logging is enabled, but endpoint-level monitoring is not configured.
